<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madam C. J. Walker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sarah Breedlove</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Madam C. J. Walker, born Sarah Breedlove near Delta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, in 1867, was an American entrepreneur, philanthropist and activist who made her fortune with a line of cosmetics and hair care products for Black women. The first child of her family born into freedom, she was orphaned at seven and worked as a domestic servant and laundress before developing her own products in Denver from 1905. In 1910 she moved to Indianapolis, home of the Madam C. J. Walker Manufacturing Company, which employed several thousand women as sales agents between 1911 and 1919. She gave to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a> and other causes, and at her death in 1919 was considered the wealthiest African-American businesswoman in America.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madam-c-j-walker.jpg" alt="Madam C. J. Walker" width="330" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Addison N. Scurlock / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madam_C._J._Walker_by_Addison_N._Scurlock.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
